2 TITL Biography
1 NOTE He was conqueror of the Persian Empire, and one of the greatest military 
2 CONT geniuses of all times. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aristotle was Alexander's tutor; he gave Alexander a thorough training in 
2 CONT rhetoric and literature and stimulated his interest in science, medicine, 
2 CONT and philosophy. In the summer of 336 bc Philip was assassinated, and 
2 CONT Alexander ascended to the Macedonian throne. He found himself surrounded 
2 CONT by enemies at home and threatened by rebellion abroad. Alexander disposed 
2 CONT quickly of all conspirators and domestic enemies by ordering their 
2 CONT execution. Then he descended on Thessaly, where partisans of independence 
2 CONT had gained ascendancy, and restored Macedonian rule. Before the end of the 
2 CONT summer of 336 bc he had reestablished his position in Greece and was 
2 CONT elected by a congress of states at Corinth. In 335 bc as general of the 
2 CONT Greeks in a campaign against the Persians, originally planned by his 
2 CONT father, he carried out a successful campaign against the defecting 
2 CONT Thracians, penetrating to the Danube River. On his return he crushed in a 
2 CONT single week the threatening Illyrians and then hastened to Thebes, which 
2 CONT had revolted. He took the city by storm and razed it, sparing only the 
2 CONT temples of the gods and the house of the Greek lyric poet Pindar, and 
2 CONT selling the surviving inhabitants, about 8000 in number, into slavery. 
2 CONT Alexander's promptness in crushing the revolt of Thebes brought the other 
2 CONT Greek states into instant and abject submission. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander began his war against Persia in the spring of 334 bc by crossing 
2 CONT the Hellespont (modern Dardanelles) with an army of 35,000 Macedonian and 
2 CONT Greek troops; his chief officers, all Macedonians, included Antigonus, 
2 CONT Ptolemy, and Seleucus. At the river Granicus, near the ancient city of 
2 CONT Troy, he attacked an army of Persians and Greek mercenaries totaling 
2 CONT 40,000 men. His forces defeated the enemy and, according to tradition, 
2 CONT lost only 110 men; after this battle all the states of Asia Minor 
2 CONT submitted to him. In passing through Phrygia he is said to have cut with 
2 CONT his sword the Gordian knot. Continuing to advance southward, Alexander 
2 CONT encountered the main Persian army, commanded by King Darius III, at Issus, 
2 CONT in northeastern Syria. The size of Darius's army is unknown; the ancient 
2 CONT tradition that it contained 500,000 men is now considered a fantastic 
2 CONT exaggeration. The Battle of Issus, in 333, ended in a great victory for 
2 CONT Alexander. Cut off from his base, Darius fled northward, abandoning his 
2 CONT mother, wife, and children to Alexander, who treated them with the respect 
2 CONT due to royalty. Tyre, a strongly fortified seaport, offered obstinate 
2 CONT resistance, but Alexander took it by storm in 332 after a siege of seven 
2 CONT months. Alexander captured Gaza next and then passed on into Egypt, where 
2 CONT he was greeted as a deliverer. By these successes he secured control of 
2 CONT the entire eastern Mediterranean coastline. Later in 332 he founded, at 
2 CONT the mouth of the Nile River, the city of Alexandria, which later became 
2 CONT the literary, scientific, and commercial center of the Greek world. 
2 CONT Cyrene, the capital of the ancient North African kingdom of Cyrenaica, 
2 CONT submitted to Alexander soon afterward, extending his dominion to 
2 CONT Carthaginian territory. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 331 Alexander made a pilgrimage to the great temple and 
2 CONT oracle of Amon-Ra, Egyptian god of the sun, whom the Greeks identified 
2 CONT with Zeus. The earlier Egyptian pharaohs were believed to be sons of 
2 CONT Amon-Ra; and Alexander, the new ruler of Egypt, wanted the god to 
2 CONT acknowledge him as his son. The pilgrimage apparently was successful, and 
2 CONT it may have confirmed in him a belief in his own divine origin. Turning 
2 CONT northward again, he reorganized his forces at Tyre and started for Babylon 
2 CONT with an army of 40,000 infantry and 7000 cavalry. Crossing the Euphrates 
2 CONT and the Tigris rivers, he met Darius at the head of an army of unknown 
2 CONT size, which, according to the exaggerated accounts of antiquity, was said 
2 CONT to number a million men; this army he completely defeated in the Battle of 
2 CONT Guagamela, on October 1, 331. Darius fled as he had done at Issus and was 
2 CONT later slain by two of his own generals. Babylon surrendered after 
2 CONT Gaugamela, and the city of Susa with its enormous treasures was soon 
2 CONT conquered. Then, in midwinter, Alexander forced his way to Persepolis, the 
2 CONT Persian capital. After plundering the royal treasuries and taking other 
2 CONT rich booty, he burned the city during a drunken binge and thus completed 
2 CONT the destruction of the ancient Persian Empire. His domain now extended 
2 CONT along and beyond the southern shores of the Caspian Sea, including modern 
2 CONT Afghanistan and Balochistan, and northward into Bactria and Sogdiana, the 
2 CONT modern Russian Turkestan, also known as Central Asia. It had taken 
2 CONT Alexander only three years, from the spring of 330 bc to the spring of 327 
2 CONT bc, to master this vast area. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In order to complete his conquest of the remnants of the Persian Empire, 
2 CONT which had once included part of western India, Alexander crossed the Indus 
2 CONT River in 326 bc, and invaded the Punjab as far as the river Hyphasis 
2 CONT (modern Beas); at this point the Macedonians rebelled and refused to go 
2 CONT farther. He then constructed a fleet and passed down the Indus, reaching 
2 CONT its mouth in September 325 bc. The fleet then sailed to the Persian Gulf. 
2 CONT With his army, he returned overland across the desert to Media. Shortages 
2 CONT of food and water caused severe losses and hardship among his troops. 
2 CONT Alexander spent about a year organizing his dominions and completing a 
2 CONT survey of the Persian Gulf in preparation for further conquests. He 
2 CONT arrived in Babylon in the spring of 323 bc. In June he contracted a fever 
2 CONT and died. He left his empire, in his own words, "to the strongest"; this 
2 CONT ambiguous testament resulted in dire conflicts for half a century. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander was one of the greatest generals of all time, noted for his 
2 CONT brilliance as a tactician and troop leader and for the rapidity with which 
2 CONT he could traverse great expanses of territory. He was usually brave and 
2 CONT generous, but could be cruel and ruthless when politics demanded. The 
2 CONT theory has been advanced that he was actually an alcoholic having, for 
2 CONT example, killed his friend Clitus (died 328 bc) in a drunken fury. He 
2 CONT later regretted this act deeply. As a statesman and ruler he had grandiose 
2 CONT plans; according to many modern historians he cherished a scheme for 
2 CONT uniting the East and the West in a world empire, a new and enlightened 
2 CONT "world brotherhood of all men." He trained thousands of Persian youths in 
2 CONT Macedonian tactics and enrolled them in his army. He himself adopted 
2 CONT Persian manners and married Eastern wives; and he encouraged and bribed 
2 CONT his officers to take Persian wives. Shortly before he died, Alexander 
2 CONT ordered the Greek cities to worship him as a god. Although he probably 
2 CONT gave the order for political reasons, he was, in his own view and that of 
2 CONT his contemporaries, of divine birth. The order was largely nullified by 
2 CONT his death shortly after he issued it. 
2 CONT
2 CONT To bind his conquests together, Alexander founded a number of cities, most 
2 CONT of them named Alexandria, along his line of march; these cities were well 
2 CONT located, well paved, and provided with good water supplies. Greek veterans 
2 CONT from his army settled in them; young men, traders, merchants, and scholars 
2 CONT were attracted to them; Greek culture was introduced; and the Greek 
2 CONT language became widely known. Thus, Alexander vastly extended the 
2 CONT influence of Greek civilization and prepared the way for the kingdoms of 
2 CONT the Hellenistic period and the conquests of the Roman Empire.
1 NOTE Alexander the Great
2 CONT 356-323 B.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT Far away in Macedon Philip 11 (382-336 B.C.) becomes king. He gatherstogether a large force of infantry and the phalanx to support his cavalryand looks eastward, fired by ambition, to free Asia Minor of the Persianking.
2 CONT
2 CONT He marries Olympias, the wild, witch-like daughter of the king of Epirus.According to Plutarch in his Life of Alexander (2.3-4) when newly wed,Philip comes upon his wife asleep with a serpent by her side. He isfilled with revulsio
2 CONC n and fears her as an enchantress.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander, born of their union, is a fair-skinned handsome youth, quickto anger. He studies under Aristotle, the most celebrated philosopher ofhis time and has Leonidas as a tutor, a man of stern temperament.Alexander thus becomes
2 CONC  a great lover of all kinds of knowledge and alwaysputs Homer's Iliad with his dagger under his pillow when he sleeps.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander's faithful companion in both battle and the hunt is his horseBucephalus. Plutarch (6.1-4) records that Alexander, barely fifteen yearsof age, tames this tempestuous and unruly steed. Bucephalus is broughtbefore Philip by
2 CONC  a Thessalian who demands an exorbitant sum of thirteentalents in exchange. No sooner does an attendant attempt to mount him,the horse rears up and tosses him to the ground. As the horse is beingled away, Alexander exclaims that h
2 CONC e is able to mount him. Philip mockshis son and asks him what sum will he pay in case he is unhorsed.Alexander replies that he will pay his father the full price of thehorse. The king and his attendants burst out into loud laughte
2 CONC r.Unabashed, Alexander runs to the horse and turns him directly towards thesun, for the youth had observed that Bucephalus is afraid of the motionof his own shadow. He then leads the horse forward, stroking him gently,and with one
2 CONC  nimble leap, mounts him, lets him go at full speed andgallops away. Philip and his attendants look on in wonder. When Alexanderdismounts, according to Plutarch (6.5), Philip embraces him and says: "0,my son, look thee out a kingd
2 CONC om equal to and worthy of thyself forMacedonia is too small for thee."
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following years Philip's estrangement from Alexander's mother,Olympias, leads to other marriages. At his wedding to the youthfulCleopatra, Attalus, the bride's uncle in a drunken fit implores the godsto give the couple a la
2 CONC wful heir to the kingdom. Alexander is outraged bythis affront and throws his drinking cup at Attalus' head. When Philiprises in anger with his sword drawn to attack his son, his foot slips andhe falls to the ground. Plutarch (9.4
2 CONC -5) records that Alexander saysinsultingly: "See there, the man who makes preparations to pass out ofEurope into Asia, overturned in passing from one seat to another."
2 CONT
2 CONT After this incident Alexander and his mother withdraw from Philip'scourt. The sullen and jealous queen travels to Epirus, Alexander toIllyria. Friends of the family bring about a reconciliation, althoughshort lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT After subjugating his neighbors, Philip crosses into central Greece. In337 he is in the Peloponnesus where he holds a congress of the Greekstates at the Isthmus. A Hellenic league is organized that acknowledgesPhilip in the milita
2 CONC ry command and furnishes contingents for anexpedition against Persia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 336 Philip is murdered during the marriage festivities of his daughterin Aegae, Macedon. He leaves behind him a kingdom beset by troubles, butat the same time, the Macedonian army that enables his son within tenyears to change 
2 CONC the face of the old World.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander is barely twenty years old when Philip is murdered. Thecountries surrounding Macedonia want to free themselves of its rule. TheGreek cities are on the verge of rebellion. Alexander puts down therevolts and at the general
2 CONC  assembly at the Isthmus, the Greek citiesagree to join him in the war against Persia and proclaim him theirgeneral.
2 CONT
2 CONT Public officials and philosophers come from all parts of the land tocongratulate Alexander -- all but Diogenes of Sinope who is living at thetime in Corinth. According to Plutarch (14.1-2) he does not even botherto leave Cranium, 
2 CONC the suburb where Alexander finds him lying in the sun.When the philosopher sees so much company about him, he raises himself alittle and glances at Alexander who asks him kindly whether he wantsanything. "Yes", Diogenes replies, "
2 CONC I would have you stand from betweenme and the sun." Alexander is struck by this answer and is so impressedby the man that, as he goes away, he tells his followers were he notAlexander, he would choose to be Diogenes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander's aim is to strike at the heart of the Persian empire andultimately conquer the entire East. He crosses the Hellespont into Asiaand at Troy sacrifices to Athena, goddess of wisdom, and honors thememories of the heroes bu
2 CONC ried there.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Persian advance guard is encamped on the further bank of the Granicusriver. Except for a few hand-picked soldiers and a body of Greekmercenaries, the Persian king depends upon oriental recruits, large innumber but weak in figh
2 CONC ting power. Alexander crosses the river onhorseback and is met by a shower of arrows. He charges, horse againsthorse with his raised lance. While the horsemen are thus engaged, theMacedonian phalanx crosses the river. The Persians
2 CONC  take fright and fleeleaving the high roads of Asia Minor open to the young Macedonianconqueror.
2 CONT
2 CONT News of this military disaster reaches Darius. At the head of a largeforce he marches toward Cilicia to engage Alexander in battle. Theirarmies meet at Issus (near modern Alexandretta) in October 333. Alexanderfights in the foremo
2 CONC st ranks while his army closes in on the Persians,putting them to flight. Darius narrowly escapes, leaving behind hisqueen, his daughters and court officials.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now the gates of the Near East lay open before Alexander. However he doesnot pursue Darius. It is of strategic importance for Alexander to controlthe naval bases from which the Persian fleet operates. So he marchesinstead on to Ph
2 CONC oenicia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eye witness accounts of the daring exploits of Alexander unfortunately donot exist. What we know about him comes from secondary sources. Arrian(first century B.C.) refers to the works of Ptolemy, a general ofAlexander, and Aristob
2 CONC olus, whose writings are lost. Diodorus Siculus(first century B.C.) and Quintus Curtius (first century A.D.) no doubthad access to earlier histories that have been destroyed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surprisingly enough, very few likenesses of the young Macedonianconqueror have come down to us. Plutarch (4.1) records that the fineststatues of Alexander were made by Lysippus for he was the only sculptortolerated by the young ma
2 CONC n. Even the inclination of Alexander's head alittle on one side towards his left shoulder was reproduced in marble andwas imitated afterwards by the generals who succeed him in an effort toemulate him. Coins minted during Alexande
2 CONC r's reign have on the obversethe head of the god Heracles wearing the lion skin. Portraits ofAlexander only appear later on the third century B.C. coins ofLysimachus, king of Thrace. Here Alexander appears as a god wearing thesacr
2 CONC ed horns of Ammon.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Alexander moves down the coast, the Phoenician cities arepanic-stricken. The Persian fleet is manned by Phoenician crews and thekings of the Phoenician cities are at the time at sea with the fleet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Independent of each other, each city adopts a position that suits itbest. Aradus (Ruad) is the most northern of the Phoenician city states.The king's son Straton, according to Arrian (2.13.7-8), hastens towelcome him and lays on h
2 CONC is head a golden crown. He yields to Alexanderthe island of Aradus and Marathus, a great and prosperous city which liesopposite on the mainland (modern Tartous).
2 CONT
2 CONT Byblos (Gebal) surrenders without resistance. The king ruling at the timeis called Ayinel. He is away sailing with the Persian fleet. Alexanderleaves Byblos behind him and marches on to Sidon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sidon was dealt a severe blow in 351 when Artaxerxes took the city. ManySidonians perished in the flames and the memory of this disaster liveson. The city is ruled at the time by a puppet of the Persians andAlexander is determined
2 CONC  to get rid of him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hephaestion, the trustworthy companion-in-arms of Alexander, is given themission to choose a new king. He finds two Sidonians, each one is worthyto rule. However it is the custom in Sidon that the king should come fromroyal stock,
2 CONC  so the choice falls upon a man, distantly related to theroyal family. This man, modest and poor, lives in the suburbs of Sidonwhere he cultivates a small garden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hephaestion delegates the two Sidonians to bring him before Alexander.They find him, Abdalonymous by name, in his garden plucking weeds. As hestands up to greet them, the two men dismount from their horses and hailhim as king. The
2 CONC y give him royal garments to wear and accompany him toAlexander in his camp.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gazing at him steadily, Alexander tells Abdalonymous that after all theyears he has lived in poverty and privation, he will now become powerfuland rich. Quintus Curtius (4.1.24-28) records that the new king of Sidonputs out his gr
2 CONC imy, work-worn hands and replies: "These hands havingnothing, I lack nothing." Alexander is impressed by these words andleaving him to rule Sidon, he marches south to Tyre.
2 CONT
2 CONT The king of Tyre is at sea with the Persian fleet. So a delegation headedby the king's son and noblemen comes out to meet the invader. It is ofstrategic importance for Alexander to take Tyre as the city is animportant base for the
2 CONC  Persians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander uses the pretext that he wishes to enter Tyre in order tosacrifice to Heracles, for the kings of Macedon hold they are descendedfrom the god. Once Tyre is his, Alexander believes, all the Phoenicianships will desert the 
2 CONC Persian king and come over to his side.
2 CONT
2 CONT Confident in the fortifications of their island city, the Tyrians object.They realize the danger is great should Alexander enter their city. Sothey send envoys to Alexander telling him that there is a temple ofHeracles on the main
2 CONC land at Palaetyrus (old Tyre), suggesting that heoffer sacrifices to the god there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander's face reddens with anger at this affront. He threatens to jointhe island fortress to the mainland by an artificial isthmus, turn Tyreinto a peninsula and bring his powerful siege engines up to the city'swalls.
2 CONT
2 CONT That night Alexander falls asleep and has a dream. He sees Heraclesstretching out his right hand to him to lead him into the city. The seersare summoned by him at once. Tyre would be taken with great toil anddifficulty, they predi
2 CONC ct, for toil is the mark of Heracles' achievements.
2 CONT
2 CONT It takes Alexander seven months before he can enter Tyre. A strait offour stadia separates the island city from the mainland and is especiallyexposed to southwest winds. Alexander orders that large stones and treetrunks from the m
2 CONC ountains of Lebanon be brought down to the coast andcast into the sea. As long as the building of the mole is near themainland, work goes on smoothly enough but as his men get into deeperwater and nearer the city, a volley of arro
2 CONC ws fall around them shot byarchers positioned on the walls. Tyrians sail up on either side, mockingand harassing them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander orders that two towers be built on the mole equiped with siegeengines. Hides and skins cover the towers so they can not be pelted withfire darts. The Tyrians fill a large horse-transport ship with dry boughsand other com
2 CONC bustible materials. They fix two masts on the prow, eachwith a projecting arm from which is suspended a cauldron filled withbitumen, sulphur and other highly inflammable materials. The stern of thevessel is loaded with stone and s
2 CONC and and is thus depressed. In this waythe prow is elevated so it can easily glide over the mole and reach thetowers. The Tyrians wait for a wind blowing towards the mole and tow theship astern with triremes. Running the "fire-ship
2 CONC " at full speed upon themole, they set torches to the combustible materials. They dash the shipviolently against the mole and the cauldrons scatter the fiery mass inall directions. The crew of the burning ship easily swim away to 
2 CONC safety.
2 CONT
2 CONT The kings of Aradus and Byblos hear that their cities are in Alexander'shands. They promptly desert the Persian fleet and arrive with theircontingents and Sidonian triremes to side with Alexander. The kings ofCyprus learn that Dar
2 CONC ius has been defeated at Issus and sail to Sidonwith one hundred and twenty ships. Triremes arrive from Rhodes, Soli,Mallos, Lycia and a fifty-oar from Macedon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrian (2.20.3) records: "To all these Alexander let bygones be bygonessupposing that it was rather from necessity than choice that they hadjoined naval forces with the Persians."
2 CONT
2 CONT While all the ships are being prepared for battle and his siege enginesfitted for the final assault, Alexander with some of his archers andcavalry march to the Anti-Lebanon. He conquers part of the country,others readily surrender
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tyrians have no choice but to go on the offensive before Alexanderattacks. The enemy fleet must be sunk, including the ships of theirsister-cities. This is not an easy task because ships from Cyprus areblocking the mouth of th
2 CONC e "Sidonian" port, so-called because it facesnorth towards Sidon. Plans must be made in secret. So sails are spreadbefore the entrance of the harbor to hide their preparations. At middaywhen the Cypriote sailors are not on their g
2 CONC uard, the Tyrians set sailwith their bravest seafighting men and attack the surprised enemy,sinking several ships.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander is infuriated by this setback. He orders his ships at once tosea to blockade the harbor. Those on the walls of Tyre see this and trywith shouts and gestures to beckon their men to turn back. It is toolate. Wheeling their
2 CONC  ships about, the Tyrians attempt to sail back to theharbor. A few manage to get to safety but Alexander's naval forces putmost of them out of action. Some of the crew jump overboard and swim toland. This victory allows the Macedo
2 CONC nians easier access to Tyre's citywalls. The battery rams are brought up against the walls. Thefortifications on the mole are so high the Macedonians are unable toscale them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander is forced to turn south to the "Egyptian" port -- that facingEgypt -- testing the walls on his way. There, a part of the city'sfortifications have broken down. Bridges are thrown over the walls butthe Tyrians repulse the
2 CONC  attack.
2 CONT
2 CONT A great fear now arises in Tyre. Quintus Curtius (4.3.22) tells us that arumor spreads like wildfire that the god Apollo is about to leave thecity. The Tyrians bind the statue of Apollo with a chain of gold to itsbase and attach t
2 CONC he chain to the altar of Heracles, their patron god,hoping that he will hold Apollo back.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander has another dream. In it he sees a satyr mocking him at adistance and eluding his grasp when he tries to catch him. Finally aftermuch coaxing, the satyr surrenders. Plutarch (24.5) records that theseers are called in and
2 CONC  dividing the word satyros into two parts, say toAlexander plausibly enough: "Tyre (Tyros in Greek) is to be thine."
2 CONT
2 CONT The final assault is frightening. Triremes are ordered to sail both tothe "Sidonian" and "Egyptian" ports in an effort to force an entrance.Alexander's ships close in on the city from all sides and bridges arethrown over the walls
2 CONC  from the vessels. Crossing over and advancingthrough breaches in the walls, the Macedonians now easily fight off theTyrians. Both harbors are forced and the Tyrian ships are captured.
2 CONT
2 CONT A large number of Tyrians desert the walls and barricade themselves inthe Shrine of Agenor. This monument is particularly revered by the peopleof Tyre for, in legendary tradition, Agenor is their king, the father ofCadmus and Euro
2 CONC pa. According to Arrian (2.24.2) it is there thatAlexander attacks them with his bodyguards. There is a bloody massacre.The Macedonians are infuriated, Seeing themselves at last masters of thecity, they fall mercilessly on the Tyr
2 CONC ians. They are also determined toavenge the death of their companions, who when sailing from Sidonearlier, are captured by the Tyrians. These men are dragged up on thewalls, executed in full view of Alexander's forces and flung in
2 CONC to the sea.
2 CONT
2 CONT Quintus Curtius (4.2.10-12) tells us that at this time a Carthaginiandelegation is in Tyre to celebrate the annual festival ofMelkart-Heracles. The king of Tyre, Azemilcus, the chief magistrates andthe Carthaginian embassy take re
2 CONC fuge in the temple of Heracles. To themAlexander grants full pardon but he severely punishes the people of Tyre.Some thirty thousand are sold into slavery. Two thousand Tyrians,according to Quintus Curtius (4.4.17) are nailed to c
2 CONC rosses along a greatstretch of the shore.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander offers a sacrifice to Heracles and holds a procession of hisarmed forces in the city. A naval review is also held in the god's honor.The siege has lasted seven months. Diodorus Siculus (17.46.5-6) ends hisaccount of the 
2 CONC dramatic siege of Tyre by telling us that Alexandersolemnly removes the golden chains and fetters from Apollo and ordersthat henceforth the god be called Apollo "Philalexander". He rewards hismen who have distinguished themselves 
2 CONC and gives a lavish funeral for hisdead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander leaves Tyre. With the fall of Gaza to the south, the way liesopen to Egypt. Upon his arrival there, Alexander consults the oracle ofZeus Ammon and is hailed by the high priest as the son of the god.
2 CONT
2 CONT He founds the city of Alexandria at the mouth of the Nile destined to bethe new commercial and intellectual center of the East Mediterraneanworld.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 331 B.C. Alexander leaves the Mediterranean to strikeinto the heart of the Persian empire. It is near Nineveh that Dariusawaits him with a large army, hastily assembled. At the battle of ArbelaDarius is defeated a
2 CONC nd flees into Media.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander follows the Tigris River into Babylonia, the central seat ofthe Persian empire and its richest region. From there he proceeds toSusa, then to the royal city of Persepolis with its enormous treasure.There he destroys the 
2 CONC palace by fire according to the geographer Strabo(15.6), ostensibly as revenge for the burning of Greek temples by Xerxesduring the Graeco-Persian wars. Plutarch (38.1-4) gives another versionsaying that the fire is started during
2 CONC  a drunken revelry but is thenextinguished by order of Alexander who regrets the deed.
2 CONT
2 CONT What we see next is a king being chased by another king. From EcbatanaAlexander pursues Darius to the Caspian. The Persian empire is crumbling,Darius is deserted by his generals one by one and by his troops. Hiscousin, Bessus, sei
2 CONC zes this opportunity to rid himself once and for allof the Persian king. At night he and a few followers burst into Darius'tent, tie him up with ropes and carry him to his chariot and on toBactria. He hopes eventually to offer the
2 CONC  Persian king as a hostage inexchange for Alexander's recognition of him as ruler of the easternsatrapies. Alexander follows Darius in hot pursuit. Seeing he cannotescape, Bessus suddenly gallops up to the royal chariot, stabs Dar
2 CONC ius todeath and gets away. When Alexander finally catches up with his rival, hecomes into possession only of his corpse. Alexander looks down on hisfallen foe with compassion, and covers his body with his purple cloak.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually Bessus is captured and put in chains. Due to the nature of thecrime, Alexander has him sentenced by Persian judges, not by himself.Bessus is found guilty of rebellion against his king. The sentence iscruel. Bessus' nose
2 CONC  and ears are cut off and he is led to Ecbatana wherehe is crucified on a tree.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander marches through Bactria and Sogdiana putting down rebellionsand founding Greek cities. Then he crosses the Hindu Kush and proceeds toIndia. One of the principalities, situated between the Hydaspes andAscenines, is ruled 
2 CONC by Porus. Alexander crosses the Hydaspes, Porus holdsthe opposite bank with a powerful force and two hundred elephants. Duringthe battle Porus is wounded and falls into Alexander's hands. HoweverAlexander gains the fallen king as 
2 CONC a friend.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is at this time, Plutarch (61.1) tells us that Bucephalus dies,wounded in battle. Others relate that the horse dies of fatigue and oldage. Alexander is overcome with grief. On the banks of the Hydaspes Riverhe builds a city on 
2 CONC the tomb of his horse which he names Bucephalia inhis memory. When he reaches the Hyphasis River (Beas) the Macedonian armyrefuses to go farther although Alexander believes he has not much more togo to reach the ocean and the east
2 CONC ern limit of the inhabited world. He isobliged to give way and the return begins.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 323 he returns to Babylon. There he makes plans for theconstruction of a great fleet and the opening of a route by sea fromBabylon to Egypt around Arabia. In Babylon he falls ill, consumed by araging fever that do
2 CONC es not leave him. He dies towards evening on June 13,323 at the age of thirty-three.
2 CONT
2 CONT His. son by Roxana, the beautiful daughter of Oxyartes, king of Bactria,is born a short time later. The child, named Alexander "Aegus", isaccepted by the Macedonian generals as joint king with Alexander'shalf-brother, Philip Arrhi
2 CONC daeus, mentally unfit to rule. Alexander'ssuccessors use these two pathetic figures as a symbol of legitimacy tocover up their own ambitions. The day is now nearing when they can carveout a kingdom for themselves on the ruins of A
2 CONC lexander's empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT The two kings, a child and one feeble of mind, are put under theguardianship and protection of Perdiccas, Peithon and Antipater, insuccession. Upon the death of Antipater, Roxana flees with her child toEpirus seeking the protectio
2 CONC n of Olympias, Alexander's mother. She istaken there by Polyperchon, an officer close to Alexander to whomAntipater had delegated his power. From there Polyperchon accompaniesOlympias, Roxana and the boy to Macedonia. All three fa
2 CONC ll into the handsof Antipater's son, Cassander, whose ambition knows no bounds. Olympiasis put to death, young Alexander and his mother are kept under closearrest. They are murdered in 310-309 by order of Cassander. Thus thedynast
2 CONC y of Alexander the Great comes to an end with the death ofAlexander IV Aegus, his son, barely twelve years of age.
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT 00. ALEXANDER III "THE GREAT", King of Macedonian-Greece 336-323BC, =[his2nd/her 3rd] Barsine, daughter of Artabazus, a satrap, &, wife, sister ofDarius III, Shah of Persia, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 01. Aesopia = Achaeus (Achaios), Prince of Syria (d275/67) [son ofSeleucus I Nicator, King of Syria 305-280, by his 1st wife, Apama,daughter of Sitamenes, a satrap, &, wife, Apama, daughter of Pharnakes, asatrap, &, wife, Apama, d
2 CONC aughter of Artaxerxes II, Shah of Persia, son ofDarius II “Nothus”, Shah of Persia, &, wife, Parysatis, daughter ofArtaxerxes I, by his 4th wife, Andria, the Babylonian heiress], begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 02. Andromachus, a general 245, d225, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 03. Laodice [II] [sister of Achaeus, claimant of the Graeco-Macedonianthrone 213BC] = Seleucus II Callinicus, King of Syria 246-226, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 04. Antiochus III Megas, King of Syria 223-187, = Laodice, daughter ofMithridates IV, King of Pontus, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 05. Antiochus, Prince, = Laodice [IV], begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 06. Nysas = Pharnaces I, King of Pontus 190-169, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 07. Mithridates V Euergetes, King of Pontus 156-121, = Laodice, daughterof Antiochus IV Epiphanes, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 08. Mithridates VI “The Great”, King of Pontus 121-63BC = [many wives],daughter of King Ptolemy of Commagene, whose wife, Apama, was thedaughter of King Prusias II of Bithynia, whose wife, Apama, was thedaughter of King Philip V o
2 CONC f Greece [and, sister of Perseus, the lastKing of Greece before the Roman Conquest], begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 09. Cleopatra = Tigranes II “The Great”, King of Armenia 95-56BC, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. daughter = P’arnabaz II (Pharnabazes), King of Iberia [Georgia] 63-30BC, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 11. daughter = Kartam (Kart’am; K’arram), son of Prince Koudjide ofEgrisi, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 12. Aderki (Aderok), co-king of Iberia 3/1BC-AD5/55?, = daughter ofOrontes [VI], a prince of Armenia, and, his wife, daughter of Antialcidasof Bactria, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 13. Kartam, co-king of Iberia ?55-72 [dates unsure], begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 14. P’arsman I (Pharasmenes), King of Iberia ?72-87 [or AD 14/37-58 asalternative dates], begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 15. Azorki [or Mithridates II], King of Iberia ?87-103 [or ?58-106], begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 16. Hamazaspe I (Amazaspus), King of Iberia ?103-113 [or ?106-116], begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 17. P’arsman II “Kveli”, King of Iberia ?113-122 [or ?116-122] = Ghadana,daughter of Meherdates, Prince of Armenia [d AD 72/6], &, wife, Awde,daughter of King Mannos VI of Osrhoene, &, wife, daughter of King IzatesII of Adiabene, 
2 CONC &, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 18. [Rh]Adam[ist(us)], King of Iberia 129-132/5, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 19. P’arsman III, King of Iberia 132/5-182/5, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 20. daughter = Vologases II “The Great”, King of Armenia 180-191, becameArsaces XXIX Vologases V/IV, “Great-King” of Parthia 191-208, begot
2 CONT
2 CONT 21. Chosroes I “The Great”, King of Armenia 191-217 [or 222-238], begot
2 CONT
