1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Pushyamitra Shunga=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Pushyamitra Shunga (IAST: Puṣyamitra Śuṅga) (c. 185-c. 149 BC) was the founder and first ruler of the Shunga Empire in North India.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pushyamitra was originally a Senapati "General" of the Maurya Empire. In 185 BCE he assassinated the last Mauryan Emperor, Brihadratha Maurya, during an army review, and proclaimed himself King. He then performed ashvamedha and br
2 CONC ought much of North India under his rule. Inscriptions of the Shungas have been found as far as the Jalandhar in the Punjab, and the Divyavadana mentions that his rule extended as far as Sagala (Sialkot, Pakistan).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Theories of origin'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Brahmin origin/Gotra origins''
2 CONT
2 CONT Patanjali's Mahabhashya and Pāṇini's Ashtadhyayi clearly states Pushyamitra Shunga was a Brahmin from Bhardwaj Gotra. while the Harivamsa Purana called him a Kashyapa Gotriya Brahmin. This problem was solved by J.C. Ghosh by refer
2 CONC ring him a Dwaimushyayana (द्वैयमुष्यायन), a Brahmin with dual Gotras. He further mentions that one class of the Gotra was called Dwaimushyayana or dual Gotriya because they were composed of two Gotras i.e. Father's and Mother's (
2 CONC here Bharadwaj and Kaashyapa). Such Brahmins could use both of their Gotras as their identity.
2 CONT
2 CONT In "Pravar Khanda" (प्रवर खण्ड) of the Apastamba, a Śauṅgaśaiśiri (शौंग-शैशिरि) gotra is mentioned. Pushyamitra was the Brahmin who was from the Shunga of Bhardwaj Gotra and Shaishiri in Katashakha (कटशाखा) of Vishwamitra ancestry
2 CONC . Baudhayana's Śrautasūtra states the Shaishiri are from the Katashakah of Vishwamitran ancestry.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Matsya Purana, Dwaimushyayana Gotra or Dual Gotra is mentioned in Śauṅgaśaiśiri Brahmins whose Prawara are the Angiras (आंगीरस), Vaarhaspatya (वार्हस्पत्य), Bhardwaj, Maudvagalya and Śaiśirēya (शैशिरेय). The first
2 CONC  four gotra are from Āṅgīrasagaṇa (आंगीरसगण) while the fifth is definitely from another Gotra otherwise it can't be a Dual Gotra. On the other side, it is also mentioned that Prawara like Kaashyap, Avatsara (अवत्सर) and Vashistha 
2 CONC are found under the Dwamushyayana Gotra in Shaishireya Kashyap. Thus with the help of the Matsya Purana and other proofs it is proven that Shaishreya Gotra is came from the combination of Kaashyap and Vashishtha Gotra but there is
2 CONC  no name of Śaiśira (शैशिर) under Vashistha, therefore Śaiśira are definitely from Kashyapa Gotra.[6] Thus Pushyamitra Shunga was of Dwaimushyayana Gotra from both Bhardwaja and Kaashyapa lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Origin of the surname Shunga''
2 CONT
2 CONT It is written in the Harivamsa Purana for Pushyamitra Shunga that an Audbhijja Kaashyapa Dwija Senani will spread the tradition of Yajna again. In this line of Harivamsa, the word Audbhijja (ओद्भिज्ज) means plant-born. Thus accord
2 CONC ing to The Harivamsa Purana, A plant-born Kaashyapa Brahmin General will restore the Vedic tradition. In Aryan Dynasties, there was a tradition to derive their dynastic names from the various objects of nature like the Sun (Suryav
2 CONC anshi), the Moon (Chandravanshi), Fire (Agnivanshi), Vegetation (Pallava, Kadamba) etc. According to Dr. Hemchandjra Raychaudhari, Shunga Dynasty took their surname from a tree. He further gave the example of Indian dynasties like
2 CONC  Kadamba (a tree name) of Banavasi, Pallava (Sanskrit word for "branch and twig") of Kanchi and Narikela-Kramukanvaya (Narikela a Sanskrit word for the Coconut) of Champa. The meaning of "Shunga" is the fig tree in Sanskrit. So ac
2 CONC cording to him, Shungas took their dynastic name from the fig tree.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Vaishya origin''
2 CONT
2 CONT According to scholars Narhari K. Bhatt, Pran Nath Chopra and T. K. Suman Kumar, Pushyamitra became governor of Saurashtra at Girinagar (near Junagadh, Gujarat) during the time of Chandragupta Maurya.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Mauryan origin''
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Buddhist Divyavadana, Pushyamitra was lineally descended from the Mauryas. But Suresh Chandra Roy didn't accept it. According to him, Divyavadana proves that there is the gap of five generations between Ashoka and
2 CONC  Pushyamitra Shunga. But It is impossible because Pushyamitra sit on throne in 184 BC and Ashoka died in 232 BC. Moreover, names of the descendants of Ashoka were mentioned wrong in Divayavadana. According to this scripture, Kunal
2 CONC a was the son of Ashoka and Sampadi was the son of Kunala, Brihaspati was the son of Sampadi, then Vrishasena, son of Brihaspati. Brihaspati's son Vrishasena, Vrishasena's son Pushyadharma and Pushyadharma's son Pushyamitra. Also,
2 CONC  Most of the Puranas say in unison that Commander Pushyamitra got the throne after killing his master Brihadratha Maurya.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Accounts of persecution'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Buddhist Accounts''
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest reference to persecution of Buddhists by Pushyamitra Shunga is from the Sarvastivadin text of 2nd Century CE, Divyavadana and its constituent part, the Ashokavadana, which describe an account where Pushyamitra asked h
2 CONC is ministers how he could get everlasting fame. They told him as long as Buddhist Law remained, he would have to construct 84,000 stupas as King Ashoka had done. But one priest told him he could have everlasting fame by doing the 
2 CONC opposite – destroying the Buddhist religion. According to these ancient accounts, Pushyamitra Shunga then went to the Kukkutarama monastery, slaughtered monks and the organization's residence; after which he proceeded to Shakala (
2 CONC Sialkot) where he issued an edict awarding a gold piece for every head of a Buddhist monk brought to him. These accounts state the destruction continued until he arrived at the Bodhi tree. According to other accounts, Pushyamitra 
2 CONC Shunga proceeded to Shakala and offered 100 Dinaras for the head of every Buddhist monk.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Sarvastivadin-Vaibhashika text of 2nd Century CE, Vibhasha, chronicles the legend of Pushyamitra Shunga destroying stupas, demolishing 500 monasteries at the Kashmir border and slaughtering monks; supported by Kumbhandas, Yaksha
2 CONC s and demons which enhanced his power making him invincible, until he approached the Bodhi tree. According to these narratives, the guardian spirit of the Bodhi tree, Yaksha Damshtranivasin, took the form of a beautiful lady, appr
2 CONC oached the king, crushed his army and killed the king. A Mahasamghika text, the Shariputrapariprichha, translated into Chinese between 317 and 420 CE also mentions the story. The Aryamanjusrimulakalpa uses abusive terms for Pushya
2 CONC mitra Shunga, such as 'Gomimukhya' (cattle-faced) and 'Gomishanda', alluding to Vedic Sacrifices revived under the Brahmin Pushyamitra Shunga.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Tibetan Buddhist Historian, Taranatha, "the brahmana king, Pushyamitra, along with other tirthikasas, started war and thus burnt down numerous Buddhist monasteries from the madhyadesha to Jalandhara. They also kil
2 CONC led a number of vastly learned monks. As a result, within five years, the doctrine was extinct in the north (Taranatha, 1970: 121).
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Archaeological evidence supporting persecution''
2 CONT
2 CONT According to John Marshall (1955, 1975) there is evidence of damage to Buddhist establishments at Takshashila around the time of Shunga. He proposes the Sanchi stupa was destroyed by Pushyamitra Shunga, but later restored by his s
2 CONC uccessor Agnimitra. According to N.N.Ghosh (1945) the Bharhut gateway was not constructed during the time of Pushyamitra Shunga, but was constructed by his successors who had a more tolerant attitude to Buddhism, compared to Pushy
2 CONC amitra Shunga, a 'leader of Brahmanic reaction'. The destruction of Ghositarama monastery at Kaushambi, in 2nd century CE, is attributed to Pushyamitra Shunga.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to P.K.Misra, Although archaeological evidence is meager, it seems likely, that the Deorkothar stupa geographically located between Sanchi and Bharhut, was destroyed as a result of Pushyamitra Shunga's fanaticism. The ex
2 CONC posed remains at Deorkothar bear evidence of deliberate destruction datable to his reign. The three-tiered railing is damaged; railing pillars lie broken to smithereens on stone-flooring. Twenty pieces of pillar have been recovere
2 CONC d, each fragment itself fractured. The site offers no indication of natural destruction.(2001).
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Academic debate''
2 CONT
2 CONT Some historians, such as K.P.Jayaswal, H.C.Raychaudhury, R.C.Mitra and D.Devahuti, have expressed skepticism of Pushyamitra' s persecution of Buddhists. Étienne Lamotte points out if there was a fight between Pushyamitra Shunga an
2 CONC d the Yaksha Damshtranivasin and Krimisha, it is impossible to pinpoint the location. Devahuti agrees with Lamotte and adds the tale of Pushyamitra offering dinaras for heads of Buddhist monks and that of his army suddenly being d
2 CONC estroyed is manifestly wrong. Mitra opines the account of the Tibetan Buddhist Historian Taranatha is absurd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Additionally, H.C.Raychaudhury and Romila Thapar, do not believe in the persecution theory; with Raychaudhury pointing out the ban by Ashoka on animal sacrifices applied not only to yagnas but also to others. Pushyamitra Shunga's 
2 CONC death strongly points to a coup d'état and Thapar points out there was no Brahmanic revolution. A common feature is lack of archaeological evidence. Romila Thapar writes that archaeological evidence casts doubt on the claims of Bu
2 CONC ddhist persecution by Pushyamitra. Koenraad Elst is also of opinion that accounts linking Pushyamitra Shunga to Buddhist persecution are untrue.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ashokavadana legend is likely a Buddhist version of Pushyamitra's attack on the Mauryas, reflecting the declining influence of Buddhism in the Shunga Imperial court. The very same Ashokavadana attributes similar cruelty to Ash
2 CONC oka against the Ājīvikas :
2 CONT
2 CONT ''At that time, an incident occurred which greatly enraged the king. A follower of the Nirgrantha (Mahâvîra) painted a picture, showing Buddha prostrating himself at the feet of the Nirgrantha. Ashoka ordered all the Ajivikas of P
2 CONC undravardhana (North Bengal) to be killed. In one day, eighteen thousand Ajivikas lost their lives. A similar kind of incident took place in the town of Pataliputra. A man who painted such a picture was burnt alive with his family
2 CONC . It was announced that whoever would bring to the king the head of a Nirgrantha would be rewarded with a dînâra (a gold coin). As a result of this, thousands of Nirgranthas lost their lives.''
2 CONT
2 CONT However, more recently, Giovanni Verardi has criticized ideological claims trying to downplaying archeological and textual evidence of brahmanic religious persecutions. Specifically those carried on by Pushyamitra. Though, support
2 CONC  of Buddhism by the Shungas at some point is suggested by an epigraph on the gateway of Bharhut, which mentions its erection "during the supremacy of the Shungas",; it is however contested by N.N.Ghosh who says the Bharhut gateway
2 CONC  was constructed by Pushymitra Shunga's successors who had a more tolerant attitude to Buddhism. Sir John Marshall noted that the Sanchi stupa was vandalized during the 2nd century before it was rebuilt later on a larger scale, su
2 CONC ggesting the original brick stupa was destroyed by Pushyamitra and then restored by his successor Agnimitra. Similarly, the Deokothar Stupas (geographically located between Sanchi and Barhut) suffered destruction during the same p
2 CONC eriod, also suggesting some kind of involvement of Shunga rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Danver, the only thing that can be said with certainty is that Pushyamitra Shunga might have withdrawn royal patronage of Buddhist institutions. With patronage shifting from Buddhism to Brahmanism, the Buddhists sided
2 CONC  with Shunga's enemies, the Indo-Greeks. Historian H.Bhattacharya and colleagues, opine that as a result, Pushyamitra Shunga put down Buddhists with a heavy hand. Therefore, if Buddhists monasteries were pillaged, the motive may b
2 CONC e construed as a political one, rather than a religious one. Danver points out that after Shunga Dynasty ended, Buddhism flourished under the Kushanas and the Shakas; and hence Buddhism did not suffer any real set-back due to the 
2 CONC Shunga Dynasty. Lamotte says it was the Vishnuite Propaganda that led Buddhism into danger.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Manusmriti''
2 CONT
2 CONT According to P.V. Kane there was a dharmashastra attributed to Swayambhu Manu, before the 4th century BC and a rajadharma attributed to Pracetasa Manu; references to which can be found in the Mahabharata and the Arthashastra. Roy 
2 CONC says these works were recast by Bhrgu between the 2nd century BC and 2nd century CE, a dating accepted by Keith. This period coincides with the reign of Pushyamitra Shunga. According to Witzel, Manudharma was first collected under
2 CONC  Pushyamitra Shunga in 150 BC. Olivelle believes Manusmriti was composed by a single individual or by a group under the chairmanship of a strong personality; and rejects the view that the text was a product of gradual accretions o
2 CONC ver centuries by different individuals. Tripathi, Doniger and Smith accept Manusmriti's dating is around beginning of the Christian era. Olivelle asserts the text was composed and written between first century BC and second centur
2 CONC y CE. The text is generally believed to be a Brahmanical response and reaction to the rise of Buddhism and Jainism; wherein 'Manu' over-emphasizes the role of dharma in state-craft in a particular sense of social conduct and legal
2 CONC  rules.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Succession of the throne'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Pushyamitra Shunga was succeeded in 151 BC by his son Agnimitra.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pushyamitra_Shunga
