1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Asharid-apal-EkurFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopediaJump to: navigation, search Ašarēd-apil-Ekur, inscribed ma-šá-rid-A-É.KUR[i 1] or mSAG.KAL-DUMU.UŠ-É.KUR[i 2] and variants,[i 3] meaning “the heir of the Ekur i
2 CONC s foremost,” was the son and successor of Tukultī-apil-Ešarra I as king of Assyria, reigning for just two years, 1076/5–1074 BC, during the turmoil that engulfed the end of that lengthy reign, and he was the 88th king to appear on
2 CONC  the Assyrian King List. His reign marked the elevation of the office of ummânu, “royal scribe,” and he was the first to have this recorded next to the king’s name on the Synchronistic King List,[i 4] possibly identifying the cont
2 CONC emporary redactor of this list.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] BiographyAccording to an early reading of the Synchronistic King List,[i 4] he was a contemporary of the Babylonian king Itti-Marduk-balāṭu, ca. 1140–1132 BC, where this monarch had perhaps been relocated to follow Marduk-n
2 CONC ādin-aḫḫē, ca. 1099-1082 BC.[2] This part of the cuneiform text is now lost[3]:448 or disproven.[3]:267 Current theories of chronological succession suggest Marduk-šāpik-zēri, ca. 1082–1069 BC, may have been his Babylonian counter
2 CONC part, with Rowton suggesting synchronizing the two year reign of Ašarēd-apil-Ekur with this king's 5th and 6th years.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT There are no royal inscriptions known from his reign and he appears only in later king lists and in an eponym list.[i 5][5] He was succeeded by his brother Aššur-bel-kala, then his nephew Eriba-Adad II, then his other brother Šamš
2 CONC i-Adad IV http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asharid-apal-Ekur
