2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantine_IX_Monomachos ''[52 languages]''
2 CONT '''Constantine IX Monomachos''', Latinized as '''Monomachus''' (Medieval Greek: Κωνσταντῖνος Θ΄ Μονομάχος, romanized: Kōnstantinos IX Monomachos; c. 1000 – 11 January 1055), reigned as Byzantine emperor from 11 June 1042 to 11 Jan
2 CONC uary 1055. He had been chosen by Zoë Porphyrogenita as a husband and co-emperor in 1042, although he had been exiled for conspiring against her previous husband, Emperor Michael IV the Paphlagonian. They ruled together until Zoë d
2 CONC ied in 1050.
2 CONT
2 CONT During Constantine's reign, the Byzantine Empire fought wars against groups which included the Kievan Rus' and the Seljuq Turks. In the year before his death, the split between the Eastern Orthodox and Roman Catholic Churches took
2 CONC  place.  
2 CONT
2 CONT # [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantine_IX_Monomachos#Early_life '''Early life'''] 
2 CONT # [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantine_IX_Monomachos#Reign '''Reign''']
2 CONT # [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantine_IX_Monomachos#Architecture_and_art '''Architecture and art''']
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT -https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantin_IX#Unions_et_post%C3%A9rit%C3%A9<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT #Constantin IX a épousé en premières noces une noble, fille d'un membre distingué de la cour impériale, qui meurt de maladie avant 1025, sans postérité95.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Veuf, il s'est remarié avec Pulchérie Sklèros, fille de Basile Sklèros, aveuglé et exilé en 1033, et par sa mère Pulchéria Argyropoulina, nièce maternelle de l’empereur Romain III Argyre, décédée avant 1034, qui est sans doute la
2 CONC  mère de sa fille unique :
2 CONT
2 CONT ##Probablement prénommée [https://www.geni.com/people/Monomakhina-of-Byzantium/6000000003858589577 Marie, mais parfois connue sous le nom d'Irène ou d'Anne], elle meurt en 1067. Elle épouse en 1046 le [https://www.geni.com/people/
2 CONC Vsevolod-Yaroslavich-Grand-Prince-of-Kiev/6000000001610448055 prince Vsevolod Ier de Kiev, grand-duc de Kiev], à la suite de l'accord de paix entre la Rus' de Kiev et l'Empire96,97.
2 CONT
2 CONT #À la suite de la mort de sa seconde épouse, il hésite à contracter une troisième union (toujours réprouvée par l'Église byzantine) et vit maritalement avec une nièce de sa défunte femme, Maria Skleraina (morte après 1044 ou entre
2 CONC  1042-1050).
2 CONT
2 CONT #Après avoir épousé en 1042 l'impératrice Zoé Porphyrogénète, il réussit à obtenir de cette dernière le maintien de sa maîtresse au palais. L'impératrice, pour le moins très complaisante, officialise ce ménage à trois en accordant
2 CONC  à la Skleraina le titre inédit de « sébaste ». Elle l'autorise en outre à participer au conseil en vertu d'un « contrat d'amitié ».
2 CONT
2 CONT #Après les disparitions de Maria Skleraina et de l'impératrice Zoé en 1050, Constantin IX tombe amoureux en 1054 de la princesse géorgienne Gorandouxt, désormais appelée Irène, sœur du roi Bagrat IV de Géorgie, otage à Constantino
2 CONC ple, qui devient sa maîtresse98.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BYZANTIUM.htm#KonstantinosIXdied1055<br/>
2 CONT a)         KONSTANTINOS Monomachos ([1005/1010]-11 Jan 1055, bur Monastery of Mangana).  Psellos names "Constantine the son of Theodosius…the last scion of the ancient family of the Monomachi in the male line" when recording that 
2 CONC Empress Zoe chose to marry him[1655].  His birth date is estimated from Psellos describing him as a "young man" at the time of the accession of Emperor Romanos III[1656].  Although well-born and held in respect, neither Emperor Ba
2 CONC sileios II nor Emperor Konstantinos VIII promoted him to office, for they were suspicious about his relations with the Skleros family after his second marriage[1657].  Nevertheless, Konstantinos appears to have enjoyed a close rel
2 CONC ationship with Empress Zoe, especially during the reign of Emperor Mikhael IV, although the latter fabricated charges against him and exiled him to the island of Lesbos.  After her joint accession with her sister, Empress Zoe reca
2 CONC lled Konstantinos from exile and married him, despite the Byzantine church's prohibition of third marriages[1658].  He was crowned 12 Jun 1042 as Emperor KONSTANTINOS IX.  He started his reign with another flush of largesse, which
2 CONC  exhausted his treasury[1659].  His general Giorgios Maniakis recaptured the eastern part of Sicily from the Arabs, but was ordered to withdraw by the emperor before he could push his advantage further.  Resentful of this treatmen
2 CONC t, Maniakis rebelled and was acclaimed emperor by his troops, but was killed in battle in 1043 en route to Constantinople.  Leon Tornikios (who had courted Emperor Konstantinos's sister Euprepia) rebelled, was proclaimed emperor S
2 CONC ep 1047, and besieged Constantinople but he was captured and blinded[1660].  After several years of lengthy dispute over the standardisation of the liturgy, the Patriarch Mikhael Cerularios in 1055 excommunicated the papal legates
2 CONC  in Constantinople (who had just pronounced his own excommunication) and triggered the final separation of the Orthodox church from the Roman Catholic.  Emperor Konstantinos suffered from a debilitating illness which attacked his 
2 CONC joints and produced partial paralysis[1661].  Cedrenus records the burial of Emperor Konstantinos the day he died "in Manganis"[1662].  
2 CONT
