1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_VII_Doukas '''Michael VII Doukas 'Parapinakēs'''']
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael VII Doukas or Ducas (Greek: Μιχαήλ Ζ΄ Δούκας, Mikhaēl VII Doukas), nicknamed Parapinakēs, Byzantine emperor from 1071 to 1078.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael VII was the eldest son of Constantine X Doukas and Eudokia Makrembolitissa, and was born c. 1050, in Constantinople. He had been associated with his father on the throne late in 1059, together with or shortly before his ne
2 CONC wly born brother Konstantios. When Constantine X died in 1067, Michael VII was 17 years old and should have been able to rule by himself. He exhibited little interest in politics, and his mother Eudokia and uncle John Doukas gover
2 CONC ned the empire as effective regents.
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 1, 1068, Eudokia married the general Romanos Diogenes, who now became senior co-emperor alongside Michael VII, Konstantios, and another brother, Andronikos. When Romanos IV was defeated and captured by Alp Arslan of the
2 CONC  Seljuk Turks at the Battle of Manzikert in August 1071, Michael VII remained in the background, while the initiative was taken by his uncle John Doukas and his tutor Michael Psellos. They conspired to keep Romanos from regaining 
2 CONC power after his release from captivity. After the dispatch of Eudokia to a monastery, Michael VII was crowned again on October 24, 1071 as senior emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although still advised by Michael Psellos and John Doukas, Michael VII became increasingly reliant on his finance minister Nikephoritzes. The emperor's chief interests, shaped by Psellos, were in academic pursuits, and he allowed 
2 CONC Nikephoritzes to increase both taxation and luxury spending without properly financing the army. In dire straits, imperial officials resorted to property confiscations and even expropriated some of the wealth of the church. The un
2 CONC derpaid army tended to mutiny, and the Byzantines lost Bari, their last possession in Italy, to the Normans of Robert Guiscard in 1071. Simultaneously, they faced a serious revolt in the Balkans, where they faced an attempt for th
2 CONC e restoration of the Bulgarian state in the same year. Although this revolt was suppressed by the general Nikephoros Bryennios, the Byzantine Empire was unable to recover its losses in Asia Minor.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Manzikert the Byzantine government sent a new army to contain the Seljuk Turks under Isaac Komnenos, a brother of the future emperor Alexios I Komnenos, but this army was defeated and its commander captured in 1073. The prob
2 CONC lem was made worse by the desertion of the Byzantines' western mercenaries, who became the object of the next military expedition in the area, led by the Caesar John Doukas. This campaign also ended in failure and its commander wa
2 CONC s likewise captured by the enemy. The victorious mercenaries now forced John Doukas to stand as pretender to the throne. In this situation Michael VII's government was forced to recognize the conquests of the Seljuk Turks in Asia 
2 CONC Minor in 1074, and to seek their support. A new army under Alexios Komnenos, reinforced by Seljuk troops sent by Malik Shah I, finally defeated the mercenaries and captured John Doukas in 1074.
2 CONT
2 CONT These misfortunes caused widespread dissatisfaction, exacerbated by the devaluation of the currency, which gave the emperor his nickname Parapinakēs, "minus a quarter". In 1078 two generals, Nikephoros Bryennios and Nikephoros Bot
2 CONC aneiates, simultaneously revolted in the Balkans and Anatolia, respectively. Nikephoros Botaneiates gained the support of the Seljuk Turks and first reached Constantinople. Michael VII resigned the throne with hardly a struggle on
2 CONC  March 31, 1078 and retired into the monastery of Stoudios. He later became metropolitan archbishop of Ephesus and died in Constantinople in c. 1090.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael VII Doukas had married Maria of Alania, daughter of King Bagrat IV of Georgia. By her he had at least one son:
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine Doukas, co-emperor from c. 1075 to 1078 and from 1081 to 1087/8, died c. 1095.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Miguel VII Ducas, emperador de Bizancio (1067 - 1078). Se le conoció también como Miguel Parapinaces ("menos de un cuarto"), por la devaluación que durante su reinado sufrió la moneda bizantina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miguel VII era hijo del emperador Constantino X y de su esposa Eudocia. Al morir su padre el 21 de mayo de 1067, Eudocia asumió la regencia del Imperio. Poco después contrajo matrimonio con Romano IV Diógenes, que fue coronado nue
2 CONC vo emperador el 1 de enero de 1068.
2 CONT
2 CONT Romano IV fue derrotado por los selyúcidas en la batalla de Manzikert (1071) (en gran parte debido a la escasa belicosidad demostrada por el primo de Miguel, Andrónico Ducas, lo que ha hecho pensar a los historiadores en una consp
2 CONC iración de la aristocrática familia de los Ducas para acabar con el poder de Romano IV). El césar Juan Ducas, padre de Andrónico, consiguió que su sobrino Miguel fuese proclamado emperador y coronado en Santa Sofía, en tanto que s
2 CONC u madre, Eudocia, era recluida en un convento. Los ejércitos mandados por Juan y Andrónico vencieron a los restos de las tropas derrotadas de Romano IV, quien fue cegado, y murió poco después a consecuencia de sus heridas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miguel VII se negó a aceptar el tratado que su predecesor, Romano IV, había firmado con el sultán selyúcida, Alp Arslan. Como consecuencia, las invasiones turcas continuaron y en 1080 se constituyó el sultanato de Rüm, que dominab
2 CONC a la mayor parte de Anatolia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Durante el reinado de Miguel, la situación del Imperio se fue progresivamente deteriorando: además de las incursiones selyúcidas, aumentó el hostigamiento en Occidente de pechenegos y magiares, la inflación aumentó considerablemen
2 CONC te (origen del apodo del emperador, Parapinaces, "menos de un cuarto"), y se produjeron varias insurrecciones militares.
2 CONT
2 CONT La primera fue la del mercenario normando Roussel de Bailleul, quien fundó un estado independiente en Anatolia, aunque fue derrotado por el general y futuro emperador Alejo Comneno. Más adelante, en noviembre de 1077, se levantó c
2 CONC ontra Miguel Nicéforo Brienio, que fue aclamado emperador en Adrianópolis, y, en Oriente, condujo una nueva insurrección Nicéforo Botaniates, strategos del thema de Anatolikon. Al mismo tiempo, estallaron revueltas en Constantinop
2 CONC la, y Miguel se vio obligado a abdicar y refugiarse al monasterio de Studion. Nicéforo Botaniates entró triunfalmente en la capital, e hizo cegar a su rival Nicéforo Brienio.
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/234811578/michael_vii-doukas
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael VII Doukas was Byzantine emperor from 1071 to 1078.
2 CONT
2 CONT Third Byzantine Emperor under the Doukid Dynasty.
