1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nizam_al-Mulk '''Wikipedia''']
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Abu Ali Hasan ibn Ali Tusi''' (April 10, 1018 – October 14, 1092), better known by his honorific title of Nizam al-Mulk (Persian: نظام‌الملک‎, "Order of the Realm") was a Persian[2][3] scholar and vizier of the Seljuq Empire. H
2 CONC e held near absolute power for 20 years after the assassination of Alp Arslan in 1072.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Early life and service to the Ghaznavids
2 CONT * 2 Service to the Seljuqs
2 CONT * 2.1 Reign of Tughril and Alp Arslan
2 CONT * 2.2 Reign of Malik Shah I
2 CONT * 3 Works
2 CONT * 4 Death
2 CONT * 5 Legacy
2 CONT * 6 References
2 CONT * 7 Sources
2 CONT * 8 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT =Early life and service to the Ghaznavids=
2 CONT
2 CONT Abu Ali Hasan was born on April 10, 1018 in a small village named Radkan, near Tus, in Iran, to a dehqan family.[5][6][7][8] His father Ali ibn Ishak served as a financial officer to the Ghaznavids.[9] However, when the Seljuq Tur
2 CONC ks defeated the Ghaznavids at the Battle of Dandanaqan in 1040, and conquered Khorasan, Abu Ali Hasan's father fled to Ghazni, where Abu Ali Hasan was working within the government, and spent three years working there until he lef
2 CONC t the city.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Service to the Seljuqs=
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Reign of Tughril and Alp Arslan==
2 CONT
2 CONT Around the year of 1043, Abu Ali Hasan stopped serving the Ghaznavids and entered the service of the Seljuq Turks. He later became chief administrator of the entire Khorasan province by 1059.[10] When Tughril died childless in the
2 CONC  city of Ray, he was succeeded by his nephew Suleiman which was contested by Alp Arslan, both of them sons of his brother Chaghri. His cousin Kutalmish who had both been a vital part of his campaigns and later a supporter of Yinal
2 CONC 's rebellion also put forth a claim. Alp Arslan, with the aid of Abu Ali Hasan, defeated Kutalmish and succeeded him on April 27, 1064.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Alp Arslan had consolidated his power in the Sejluq realm, he appointed Abu Ali Hasan as his vizier who would remain in that position throughout the reigns of Alp Arslan (1063–1072) and Malik Shah I (1072–1092). Abu Ali Hasa
2 CONC n was also given the title of "Nizam al-Mulk" ("Order of the Realm").
2 CONT
2 CONT Alp Arslan's strength lay in the military realm. Domestic affairs were handled by Nizam al-Mulk, who also founded of the administrative organization that characterized and strengthened the sultanate during the reigns of Alp Arslan
2 CONC  and his son, Malik Shah I. Military fiefs, governed by Seljuq princes, were established to provide support for the soldiery and to accommodate the nomadic Turks to the established Anatolian agricultural scene. This type of milita
2 CONC ry fiefdom enabled the nomadic Turks to draw on the resources of the sedentary Iranians, Turks, and other established cultures within the Seljuq realm, and allowed Alp Arslan to field a huge standing army without depending on trib
2 CONC ute from conquest to pay his soldiers. He not only had enough food from his subjects to maintain his military, but the taxes collected from traders and merchants added to his coffers sufficiently to fund his continuous wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nizam accompanied Alp Arslan in all his campaigns and journeys, except a few. On February/March 1064 Alp Arslan, along with his son Malik Shah I and Nizam al-Mulk, campaigned in Byzantine Armenia, where they managed to capture Ani
2 CONC . Several minor rulers then acknowledged Seljuq authority, while Alp Arslan and Nizam continued to penetrate deeper into Caucasus, reaching Georgia. The Georgian ruler Bagrat IV, managed to make peace with Alp Arslan by giving his
2 CONC  niece to him in marriage.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nizam also made some expeditions on his own and conquered the citadel of Estakhr from the Shabankara chieftain Fadluya in 1067, and made another expedition in Fars. These successful conquests are said to have greatly increased his
2 CONC  reputation.[10] On August 26 of 1071, the decisive battle of Manzikert was fought, which Nizam al-Mulk was not present because he had been sent to Persia with a convoy of materials.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Reign of Malik Shah I==
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Alp Arslan's assassination in 1072, Malik Shah I was challenged in battle by his uncle, Kavurt. In January 1074, their armies met near Hamadan, Kavurt's troops consisted of the traditional Turkmen elements from Alp Arsla
2 CONC n's army, while Malik's consisted of ghulams and contingents of Kurdish and Arab troops. Due to Turkmen defections to Malik's army, Kavurt was defeated and, despite Malik's consideration for mercy, later poisoned, presumably on th
2 CONC e orders of Nizam al-Mulk.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Nizam's excellent guidance the Seljuq armies contained the Ghaznavids in Khorasan, rolled back the Fatimids in Syria, defeated other Seljuq pretenders to the throne, invaded Georgia and reduced it to a tributary state, compe
2 CONC lled the submission of regional governors, and kept the Abassid Caliphs in a position of impotence.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nizam al-Mulk left a great impact on organization of the Seljuq governmental bodies and hence the title Nizam al-Mulk which translates as "Order of the Realm". He was pivotal figure who bridged the political gap between both the A
2 CONC bbasids and the Seljuqs against their various rivals such as the Fatimids. The Seljuq military was heavily mixed of different ethnicities, which included Turks, Armenians, Greeks, Arabs, and Slavs. Nizam, however, was in more favo
2 CONC r of Iranian soldiers, such as the Dailamites, Khorasanis, and the Shabankara Kurds. He was also in favor of non-Iranian soldiers such as the Georgians.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nizam al-Mulk had many political objectives, which was:
2 CONT
2 CONT * The creation of an employment opportunity for the Turkmens, who had immigrated to the Iranian plateau during the Seljuq successes in Persia, the nomadic way of life of the Turkmens represented a significant threat to the politic
2 CONC al and economic stability of the country.
2 CONT * The demonstration of the power of the Sultan (i.e. the strength and mobility of his forces, but also his grace towards docile rebels).
2 CONT * Maintaining local Sunni and Shiite rulers as vassals of the Sultan and the increased use of relatives of the Sultan as provincial governors.
2 CONT * The prevention of a dispute over the successorship of Malik Shah I.
2 CONT * Maintaining good relations with the Abbasid Caliphate.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1081/1082, Ibn Bahmanyar, one of the many enemies of Nizam, tried to poison him, but failed and was blinded by Nizam. After the blinding of Ibn Bahmanyar, the enemies of Nizam made false stories about him and his son. This grea
2 CONC tly angered Nizam's son Jamal al-Mulk, who tore out the tongue of Ja'farak, one of the perpetrators of the false stories. Malik Shah had no power to intervene in the event, but instead had Jamal poisoned.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1091, a group of Qarmatians sacked Basra, while the Ismailis under the leadership of Hassan-i Sabbah seized the fortress of Alamut. Moreover, the succession to the sultanate was complicated by the death of two of Malik Shah's e
2 CONC ldest sons: Dawud (died 1082) and Ahmad (died 1088), whom both were sons of the Kara-Khanid Princess Tarkan Khatun, she also had a son named Mahmud (born 1087) whom she wanted to succeed his father, while Nizam and most of the Sel
2 CONC juq army was in favor of Barkiyaruq,[14] the oldest of all Malik Shah's living sons and born to a Seljuq princess. Turkan Khatun then allied with Taj al-Mulk Abu'l Ghana'im to try to remove Nizam from his post. Taj even accused Ni
2 CONC zam of corruption before the sultan. Malik Shah I, however, did not dare to dismiss Nizam.[9] Nizam later besieged Alamut, but was forced to withdraw.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1092, Nizam, just before his death, knowing that his enemies were planning plots against him, made a famous speech at the court:
2 CONT
2 CONT >''Tell the Sultan, If you have not already realized that I am your co-equal in the work of ruling, then know that you have only attained to this power through my statesmanship and judgement. Does he not remember when his father w
2 CONC as killed, and I assumed responsibility for the conduct of affairs and crushed the rebels who reared their heads, from his own family and from elsewhere. Tell him that the stability of that regal cap is bound up with this vizieria
2 CONC l inkstand, and that the harmony of these two interests is the means of securing all objects soughts after and the ultimate cause of all objects gained. If ever I close up this inkstand, that royal power will topple.[14]''
2 CONT
2 CONT =Works=
2 CONT
2 CONT Aside from his extraordinary influence as vizier with full authority, he is also well known for systematically founding a number of schools of higher education in several cities like Baghdad, Isfahan, Nishapur, Mosul, Basra, and H
2 CONC erat, the famous Nizamiyyah schools, which were named after him. In many aspects, these schools turned out to be the predecessors and models of universities that were established in Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nizam al-Mulk is also widely known for his voluminous treatise on kingship titled Siyasatnama (The Book of Government) which was written after Malik Shah had requested that his ministers produce books on government, administration
2 CONC  and the troubles facing the nation. However, the treatise made by Nizam was the only one to receive approval and was consequently accepted as forming "the law of the constitution of the nation".[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT He also wrote a book titled Dastur al-Wuzarā, written for his son Abulfath Fakhr al-Malik, which is not dissimilar to the famous book of Qabus nama.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Death=
2 CONT
2 CONT Nizam al-Mulk was assassinated en route from Isfahan to Baghdad on 10 Ramadan 485 A.H. (14 October 1092) The mainstream literature says he was stabbed by the dagger of a member of the Assassins (Hashshashin) sent by the notorious 
2 CONC Hassan-i-Sabbah near Nahavand, as he was being carried on his litter. The killer approached him disguised as a dervish.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT This account is particularly interesting in light of a possibly apocryphal story recounted by Jorge Luis Borges. In this story a pact is formed between a young Nizam al-Mulk (at that time known as Abdul Khassem) and his two friend
2 CONC s, Omar Khayyam and Hassan-i-Sabbah. Their agreement stated that if one should rise to prominence, that they would help the other two to do likewise. Nizam al-Mulk was the first to do this when he was appointed vizier to the sulta
2 CONC n Alp Arslan. To fulfil the pact he offered both friends positions of rank within the court. Omar refused the offer, asking instead to be given the means to continue his studies indefinitely. This Nizam did, as well as building hi
2 CONC m an observatory. Although Hassan, unlike Omar, decided to accept the appointment offered to him, he was forced to flee after plotting to dispose Nizam as vizier. Subsequently, Hassan came upon and conquered the fortress of Alamut
2 CONC , from where he established the Assassins.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another report says he was killed in secret by Malik Shah I in an internal power struggle. Consequently, his murder was avenged by the vizier's loyal academics of the Nizamiyyah, by assassinating the Sultan.[17] The account is dis
2 CONC puted and remains a controversy because of the long history of friendship between Malik Shah I and Nizam.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another report says that he was assassinated with Malik Shah I in the same year, after a debate between Sunni and Shi'a scholars which was prepared by him by the orders of Malik Shah I and which resulted in converting him and the 
2 CONC king to the Shi'a ideology.[18] The story is reported by the son-in-law of Nizam al-Mulk, Mughatil ibn Bakri who attended the debate.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Legacy=
2 CONT
2 CONT Nizam al-Mulk was an excellent and clever vizier, he represented the majesty, splendor and hospitality of the Barmakids, historians and poets describe him as a great organizer and an ideal soldier and scholar.[19] Only thanks to h
2 CONC im it was possible for the Seljuq Turks to establish a powerful empire in their new home.[20] Nizam was not only the leader of the Persian-dominated bureaucratic (divan), but was also an Atabeg who served in the royal court (dadga
2 CONC r) and played an important role between the politically and culturally differences of the Iranians and Turks. He was also responsible for establishing distinctly Persian forms of government and administration which would last for 
2 CONC centuries.[21] Because of his excellent tutorship and close friendship with Malik Shah, he was usually called "father" by him.[10] He was even greatly respected by his ghulams, who, after the death of Nizam, took revenge on severa
2 CONC l of his rivals, such as Taj al-Mulk.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Even after his death his family continued to play an important role in the Seljuq Empire. All of his twelve sons held important offices in the Seljuq Empire, the most prominent of his sons were; Ahmad ibn Nizam al-Mulk, served as 
2 CONC the vizier of the Seljuq Sultan Muhammad I and the Abbasid caliph al-Mustarshid;[22] Shams al-Mulk Uthman was the governor of Merv and head of the Seljuq military; Fakhr al-Mulk served as the vizier of Barkiyaruq and Muhammad I; J
2 CONC amal al-Mulk (who died before Nizam) served as the governor of Balkh; Izz al-Mulk and Mu'ayyid al-Mulk served as the vizier of Barkiyaruq;[23] Imad al-Mulk Abu'l-Kasim served as the vizier of the Seljuq governor of Balkh.
2 CONT
