1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahmad_ibn_Nizam_al-Mulk '''Wikipedia''']
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ahmad ibn Nizam al-Mulk''' (Persian: احمد بن نظام الملک‎), was a Persian vizier of the Seljuq Empire and then the Abbasid Caliphate. He was the son of Nizam al-Mulk,[1] one of the most famous viziers of the Seljuq Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Biography
2 CONT * 2 See also
2 CONT * 3 References
2 CONT * 4 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahmad was born in Balkh, he was the son of Nizam al-Mulk, and a Georgian princess, who was the niece or daughter of Bagrat III of Georgia. During the lifetime of his father, Ahmad lived in Hamadan and Isfahan, and continued to do 
2 CONC so in a few years after his father's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1106/1107, he went to the court of Sultan Muhammad I to file a complaint against the rais (head) of Hamadan. When Ahmad arrived to the court, Muhammad I appointed him as his vizier, replacing Sa'd al-Mulk Abu'l-Mahasen Abi who 
2 CONC had been recently executed on suspicion of heresy. The appointment was due mainly to the reputation of his Ahmad's father. He was then given various titles which his father held (Qewam al-din, Sadr al-islam and Nizam al-Mulk).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahmad was vizier for four years in which he in 1107/1108 accompanied Sultan Muhammad I during his campaign in Iraq, where his army managed to defeat and kill the Mazyadid ruler Sayf al-dawla Sadaqa ibn Mansur, who bore the title "
2 CONC king of the Arabs". In 1109, Muhammad I sent Ahmad and Chavli Saqavu to capture the Ismaili fortresses of Alamut and Ostavand, but they failed achieve any decisive result and withdrew.[2] In 1010, a Ismaili attempted to assassinat
2 CONC e Ahmad in a mosque at Baghdad, but failed to do so. Ahmad was shortly replaced by Khatir al-Mulk Abu Mansur Maybudi as vizier of the Sejluq Empire. According to Ali ibn al-Athir, Ahmad then retired to a private life in Baghdad, b
2 CONC ut according to Anushirvan ibn Khalid, Muhammad I had Ahmad imprisoned for ten years.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1122, the son of Muhammad I, Mahmud II was ruling as the Sultan of the Seljuq Empire, with another son of Nizam al-Mulk, Shams al-Mulk Uthman, as his vizier. During the same year, the Abbasid caliph al-Mustarshid deposed and im
2 CONC prisoned his vizier Amid al-dawla Jalal al-Din Hasan ibn Ali. Mahmud II then imposed Ahmad as al-Mustarshid's vizier. Ahmad later fought against the Mazyadid Dubays ibn Sadaqa. Ahmad also fortified the walls around Baghdad.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT One year later, Mahmud II removed Shams al-Mulk Uthman as his vizier, and had him executed. The Abbasid caliph then used this opportunity to get rid of Ahmad as his vizier.[4] Ahmad then retired to a school in Baghdad which was fo
2 CONC unded by his father, the Nezamiyeh, where he lived the last 25 years of his life, dying in 1149/1150.[1]
2 CONT
