2 CONT
2 CONT David V
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Georgia, 1154–1155     George III
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Georgia, 1155–1184       Burdukhan of Alania   Rusudan     
2 CONT
2 CONT Demna         Tamar
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen of Georgia, 1184–1213     1. Yuri Bogolyubsky     ?Rusudan     
2 CONT
2 CONT                           2. David Soslan 
2 CONT
2 CONT               George IV Lasha
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Georgia, 1213–1223   Rusudan
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen of Georgia, 1223–1245       Mughis ud-din Turkan Shah of Erzincan 
2 CONT
2 CONT               David VII Ulu
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Georgia, 1247–1270         David VI Narin
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Georgia, 1245–1293   Tamar (Gürcü Hatun)   
2 CONT
2 CONT  Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ The name Tamar is of Hebrew origin and, like other biblical names, was favored by the Georgian Bagrationi dynasty because of their claim to be descended from David, the second king of Israel. Toumanoff (1940), p. 299, fn. 4.
2 CONT
2 CONT As of May 2008, Tamar was the second most popular – after Nino – female name in Georgia. (Georgian) Civil Registry of Georgia (2008-05-14), ქალთა სახელებს შორის თამარი პოპულარობით მეორე ადგილზეა ("Tamar is the second most popular 
2 CONC female name"). Retrieved on 2008-07-05. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Rapp (2003), p. 338. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ a b c d Eastmond (1998), p. 94. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Machitadze, Archpriest Zakaria (2006), "Holy Queen Tamar (†1213)", in The Lives of the Georgian Saints. pravoslavie.ru. Retrieved on 2008-07-21.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Tamar's paternal aunt was the Comnenoi's grandmother on their father’s side, as it has been conjectured by Toumanoff (1940). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ (Georgian) Shengelia, N., საქართველოს საგარეო პოლიტიკური ურთიერთობანი თამარის მეფობაში ("Foreign Relations of Georgia during the reign of Tamar"), in Melikishvili (1979). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ In the Middle Ages, the terms "Abkhazia" and "Abkhazians" were predominantly used in a wider sense, covering, for all practical purposes, the whole of western Georgia. It was not until the 15th/16th century, after the fragmentat
2 CONC ion of the unified Georgian kingdom, that these terms resumed their original, restricted sense, referring to the territory that corresponds to modern-day Abkhazia and to the ethnic group living there. Barthold, Wasil & Minorsky, V
2 CONC ladimir, "Abkhaz", in The Encyclopaedia of Islam, vol. 1, 1960. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ "Kartvelians", the modern self-designation of the Georgians, originally referred to the inhabitants of the core central Georgian province of Kartli – Iberia of the Classical and Byzantine sources. By the early 9th century, the G
2 CONC eorgian literati had expanded the meaning of "Kartli" to other areas of medieval Georgia held together by religion, culture, and language. Rapp (2003), pp. 429–430. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamar_of_Georgia [........For six years, Tamar was a co-ruler with her father upon whose death, in 1184, Tamar continued as the sole monarch and was crowned a second time at the Gelati cathedral near 
2 CONC Kutaisi, western Georgia. She inherited a relatively strong kingdom, but the centrifugal tendencies fostered by the great nobles were far from being quelled. There was considerable opposition to Tamar's succession; this was sparke
2 CONC d by a reaction against the repressive policies of her father and encouraged by the new sovereign's other perceived weakness, her sex.[12] As Georgia had never previously had a female ruler, a part of the aristocracy questioned Ta
2 CONC mar's legitimacy, while others tried to exploit her youth and supposed weakness to assert greater autonomy for themselves.[12] The energetic involvement of Tamar's influential aunt Rusudan and the Georgian Catholicos-Patriarch Mic
2 CONC hael IV Mirianisdze was crucial for legitimizing Tamar's succession to the throne.[14] However, the young queen was forced into making significant concessions to the aristocracy. She had to reward the Catholicos-Patriarch Michael'
2 CONC s support by making him a chancellor, thus placing him at the top of both the clerical and secular hierarchies.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Tamar was also pressured into dismissing her father's appointees, among them the constable Qubasar (ყუბასარი), a Georgian Kipchak of ignoble birth, who had helped George III in his crackdown on the defiant nobility.[13] One of the
2 CONC  few untitled servitors of George III to escape this fate was the treasurer Qutlu Arslan who now led a group of nobles and wealthy citizens in a struggle to limit the royal authority by creating a new council, karavi, whose member
2 CONC s would alone deliberate and decide policy.[15] This attempt at "feudal constitutionalism" was rendered abortive when Tamar had Qutlu Arslan arrested and his supporters were inveigled into submission.[13] Yet, Tamar's first moves 
2 CONC to reduce the power of the aristocratic élite were unsuccessful. She failed in her attempt to use a church synod to dismiss the Catholicos-Patriarch Michael, and the noble council, darbazi, asserted the right to approve royal decr
2 CONC ees.[15] Even the queen's first husband, the Rus' prince Yuri, was forced on her by the nobles.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Pursuant to dynastic imperatives and the ethos of the time, the nobles required Tamar to marry in order to have a leader for the army and to provide an heir to the throne.[3][13] Their choice fell on Yuri, son of the murdered prin
2 CONC ce Andrei I Bogolyubsky of Vladimir-Suzdal, who then lived as a refugee among the Kipchaks of the North Caucasus. The choice was approved by Tamar's aunt Rusudan and the prince was brought to Georgia to marry the queen in 1185.[16
2 CONC ] Yuri proved to be an able soldier, but a difficult person and he soon ran afoul of his wife.[3][13] The strained spousal relations paralleled a factional struggle at the royal court in which Tamar was becoming more and more asse
2 CONC rtive of her rights as a queen regnant.[17] The turning point in Tamar's fortunes came with the death of the powerful Catholicos-Patriarch Michael whom the queen replaced, as a chancellor, with her supporter, Anton Gnolistavisdze.
2 CONC [17] Tamar gradually expanded her own power-base and elevated her loyal nobles to high positions at the court, most notably the Zakarids-Mkhargrzeli.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1187, Tamar persuaded the noble council to approve her to divorce Yuri, who was accused of addiction to drunkenness and "sodomy" and was sent off to Constantinople.[17] Assisted by several Georgian aristocrats anxious to check 
2 CONC Tamar's growing power, Yuri made two coup attempts, but failed and went off to obscurity after 1191.[13] The queen chose her second husband herself. He was David Soslan, an Alan prince, to whom the 18th-century Georgian scholar Pr
2 CONC ince Vakhushti ascribes descent from the early 11th-century Georgian king George I.[18] David, a capable military commander, became Tamar's major supporter and was instrumental in defeating the rebellious nobles who rallied behind
2 CONC  Yuri.[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT Tamar and David had two children. In 1192 or 1194, the queen gave birth to a son, George-Lasha, the future king George IV. The daughter, Rusudan, was born c. 1195 and would succeed her brother as a sovereign of Georgia] ..........
2 CONC ...........
