1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%BCrc%C3%BC_Hatun '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gürcü Hatun''' (Georgian: გურჯი-ხათუნი) (fl. 1237-1286 C.E.) was a Georgian royal princess from Bagrationi dynasty and Queen consort of Sultanate of Rum being favorite wife of sultan Kaykhusraw II.[1] After his death in 1246 sh
2 CONC e married the Anatolian strongman Pervane. She was the mother of sultan Kayqubad II and patron to Rumi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her title Gürcü Hatun means "Georgian Lady".
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born as Tamar (Georgian: თამარი) and had a biblical name popular in Kingdom of Georgia and was named after her grandmother Queen Tamar the Great.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Gürcü Hatun was the daughter of Queen Rusudan of Georgia and the Seljuk prince Ghias ad-din, a grandson of Kilij Arslan II.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a sister of King David VI of Georgia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like most Georgians, Tamar initially remained an Eastern Orthodox Christian but is known to have converted to Islam at a later point, with no further information on how the conversion came about. It is said that the sun on the Sel
2 CONC juk coins of that time symbolizes Tamar, while the lion stands for the sultan himself. This emblem, known as shir-u hurshid (Lion and Sun), later became widespread in the Islamic world (though its origins date back to much earlier
2 CONC  times). After the death of Kaykhusraw in 1246, the government of the sultanate was seized by the Pervane Mu‘in al-Din Suleyman who married Gürcü Hatun.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is known to have patronized science and art, and to have been on friendly terms with the famous Sufi poet Rumi in particular. She also sponsored the construction of the poet’s tomb in Konya.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Cosmopolitanism and the Middle Ages, John M. Ganim, 51
2 CONT # ჯაველიძე ე., ქართული საბჭოთა ენციკლოპედია, ტ. 4, გვ. 579-580, თბ., 1979 წელი.
2 CONT # H. Crane "Notes on Saldjūq Architectural Patronage in Thirteenth Century Anatolia," Journal of the Economic and Social History of the Orient, v. 36, n. 1 (1993), p. 18.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Gürcü Hatun (Georgian: გურჯი-ხათუნი) (fl. 1237-1286?) was a Georgian princess and favorite wife of Kaykhusraw II, Seljuk Sultan of Rum. After his death in 1246 she married the Anatolian strongman Pervane Mu‘in al-Din Suleyman. She
2 CONC  was the mother of Kayqubad II and patron to Rumi. Her title Gürcü Hatun means "Georgian Lady"; her baptismal name was Tamar. Gürcü Hatun was born Princess Tamar to Queen Rusudan of Georgia and Moghis ad-Din, son of the Seljuk sul
2 CONC tan Tugrul II. Rusudan gave her daughter to Kaykhusraw in marriage to secure the peace with the Seljuks. Tamar initially remained a Christian but later embraced Islam. It is said that the sun on the Seljuk coins of that time symbo
2 CONC lizes Tamar, while the lion stands for the sultan himself. This emblem, known as shir-i hurshid (Lion and Sun), would later become widespread in the Islamic world (though its origins date back to much earlier times). After the dea
2 CONC th of Kaykhusraw in 1246, the government of the sultanate was seized by the Pervane Mu‘in al-Din Suleyman who married Gürcü Hatun.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is known to have patronized science and art, and to have been on friendly terms with the famous Sufi poet Jalal ad-Din Rumi in particular. She also sponsored the construction of the poet’s tomb in Konya.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Tamar - Gürcü Hatun (Georgian: გურჯი-ხათუნი) (fl. 1237-1286) was a Georgian royal princess from Bagrationi dynasty and Queen consort of Sultanate of Rum being favorite wife of sultan Kaykhusraw II.[1] After his death in 1246 she m
2 CONC arried the Anatolian strongman Pervane. She was the mother of sultan Kayqubad II and patron to Rumi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her title Gürcü Hatun means "Georgian Lady" in Turkic languages.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born as Tamar (Georgian: თამარი) and had a biblical name popular in Kingdom of Georgia and was named after her grandmother Queen Tamar the Great.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Gürcü Hatun was the daughter of Queen Rusudan of Georgia and the Seljuk prince Ghias ad-din, a grandson of Kilij Arslan II.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a sister of King David VI of Georgia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like most Georgians, Tamar initially remained an Eastern Orthodox Christian but is known to have converted to Islam at a later point, with no further information on how the conversion came about. It is said that the sun on the Sel
2 CONC juk coins of that time symbolizes Tamar, while the lion stands for the sultan himself. This emblem, known as shir-u hurshid (Lion and Sun), later became widespread in the Islamic world (though its origins date back to much earlier
2 CONC  times). After the death of Kaykhusraw in 1246, the government of the sultanate was seized by the Pervane Mu‘in al-Din Suleyman who married Gürcü Hatun.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is known to have patronized science and art, and to have been on friendly terms with the famous Sufi poet Rumi in particular. She also sponsored the construction of the poet’s tomb in Konya.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Gürcü Hatun was the daughter of Queen Rusudan of Georgia and the Seljuk prince Ghias ad-din, a grandson of Kilij Arslan II.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a sister of King David VI of Georgia.
2 CONT
2 CONT  It is said that the sun on the Seljuk coins of that time symbolizes Tamar, while the lion stands for the sultan himself. This emblem, known as shir-u hurshid (Lion and Sun), later became widespread in the Islamic world (though it
2 CONC s origins date back to much earlier times).
2 CONT --------------------
