1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantine_I_of_Imereti '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Constantine I''' (Georgian: კონსტანტინე I, Konstantine I; died 1327), from the House of Bagrationi, was king of the western Georgian kingdom of Imereti from 1293 to 1327.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Accession and civil war=
2 CONT
2 CONT A son of the Georgian king David VI Narin and his wife, Tamar Amanelisdze,[1] or a Palaeologian princess.[2] Constantine succeeded to the throne of Imereti upon his father's death in 1293. Unlike his eastern Georgian counterparts,
2 CONC  Constantine remained independent from the Ilkhanid hegemony, but he faced serious internal unrest as his younger brother Michael opposed his accession and seized control of the regions of Racha, Lechkhumi, and Argveti. In vain di
2 CONC d the nobles of Imereti try to reconcile the brothers and internecine conflict continued to upset the country.[3][1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The great nobles took advantage of the situation to assert their autonomy. Giorgi I Dadiani, Duke of Mingrelia, subjugated much of the duchy of Tskhumi and expanded his possessions up to Anacopia. The Shervashidze entrenched in Ab
2 CONC khazia, the Gurieli in Guria, and Vardanidze in Svaneti, showing little subservience to the royal authority.[3] Constantine died amid these disturbances in 1327, having had no children, and his brother Michael succeeded him as kin
2 CONC g.[3][1]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Culture=
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine is known to have restored Monastery of the Cross in Jerusalem to the Georgian ownership in 1305. He also had the monastery refurbished and repainted. His contributions are emphasized in a document instituting the Agape
2 CONC  feast, in his own lifetime, on 21 May, the feast day of Constantine the Great. Constantine might have been an unidentified royal person depicted on a fresco in Ienashi in Upper Svaneti. His links to that region is also known from
2 CONC  a charter issued by Constantine to the Svan Goshkoteliani clan.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Rayfield, Donald (2012). Edge of Empires: A History of Georgia. London: Reaktion Books. pp. 132, 139–140. ISBN 1780230303.
2 CONT # Toumanoff, Cyril (1949–51). "The Fifteenth-Century Bagratids and the Institution of Collegial Sovereignty in Georgia". Traditio. 7: 173.
2 CONT # Bagrationi, Vakhushti (1976). Nakashidze, N.T., ed. История Царства Грузинского [History of the Kingdom of Georgia] (PDF) (in Russian). Tbilisi: Metsniereba. p. 41.
2 CONT # Kenia, Marine (2002). "სამეფო პორტრეტი იენაშის იანის ეკლესიის მოხატულობაში" [A royal portrait in murals of the Iani Church of Ienashi]. sakartvelos sidzveleni (in Georgian and English). 2: 94–106.
