1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amalric,_Prince_of_Tyre
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalric de Lusignan or Amaury II de Lusignan, Prince of Tyre (c. 1272 – June 5, 1310, Nicosia), of the Lusignan family, was a son of Hugh III of Cyprus and Isabella of Ibelin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalric was at the Fall of Tripoli in 1289, in which he led a company of knights and four galleys from Cyprus. He escaped the siege of Tripoli together with Lucia of Tripoli, and was made Constable of Jerusalem in April 1289.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1290, he became Lord of Tyre. He was the officer in command of the Accursed Tower at the siege of Acre in 1291, and escaped the fall of the Kingdom of Jerusalem with his brother, King Henry, thus becoming Titular Constable of J
2 CONC erusalem and Titular Lord of Tyre.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1300 Amalric attempted combined military operations with the Mongols under Ghazan to retake the Holy Land. He and other Cypriots occupied the island of Ruad, but the Mongols did not appear and the Westerners withdrew, eventuall
2 CONC y losing the island entirely in the Siege of Ruad.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his brother Henry became unpopular in Cyprus, Amalric overthrew him with the aid of the Knights Templar and some of the barons, assuming the titles of "Governor and Rector" and Regent of Cyprus and Titular Regent of Jerusalem
2 CONC  on April 26, 1306. The overthrow was not violent; Henry had few supporters, and was simply taken away and confined at Strovolos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalric's rule was initially popular, and he repaired relations with Venice, Genoa, and the Hospitallers.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, when the Order of the Knights Templar was being suppressed in 1307, he was compelled to obey the Papal directive to arrest local Templars, which resulted in a small uprising in favor of Henry in January 1308. It quickly c
2 CONC ollapsed, but Amalric was forced to arrest a number of nobles, including Rupen of Montfort, John of Dampierre, and various members of the Ibelin family. In April, two of the Ibelins were exiled to Armenia, and John of Dampierre wa
2 CONC s mortally wounded by a mob after attempting to communicate with King Henry. In February 1310, Amalric sent Henry into exile in Armenia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalric was murdered by Simon of Montolif at Nicosia on June 5, 1310, and buried at Santa Sophia, at Nicosia. On his death, his brother Aimery was proclaimed Governor of Nicosia, but was soon defeated and imprisoned, and Henry res
2 CONC tored to his throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1292/1293 Amalric married Cilician Armenian princess Isabella in the city of Nicosia. They had five sons and one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh of Lusignan (died between 1318 & April 9, 1323, Cilicia), Lord of Crusoche married to Eschiva of Ibelin (died after March 1324).
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry of Lusignan (murdered before April 9, 1323, Cilicia), unmarried
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy of Lusignan (d. April 17, 1344, Armenia), King of Armenia as Constantine II
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Lusignan (murdered August 7, 1343, Cilicia), sometime Constable and Regent of Cilicia, married Sultana of Georgia (after 1343), daughter of Giorgi V "the Magnificent", King of Georgia, died 1346, children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bohemond of Lusignan (died Venice, 1364)
2 CONT
2 CONT Leon V of Armenia (bastard)
2 CONT
2 CONT Bohémond of Lusignan (murdered April 17, 1344, Cilicia), Count of Corcyus, Lord of Korikos (1336), married in 1340 Euphemia of Neghir (1325 – aft. 1381, Jerusalem), daughter of Baldwin of Neghir, Marshal of Armenia, and had a bast
2 CONC ard son: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Barthelemy of Lusignan, Co-Regent of Armenia (died after 1373), unmarried and without issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes (Mary) of Lusignan (d. aft. 1309), married c. 1305 or 1305/1306 Leo III of Armenia (1297 – murdered 1307), without children
2 CONT
2 CONT After Amalric's death, his widow and children remained in Armenia; only his daughter would die a natural death, his widow and sons all being murdered at various times.
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/280655571/amalric-of_lusignan
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalric (Amaury) of Lusignan was Lord of Tyre, and in 1289 became Constable of Jerusalem. He was a younger son of King Hugh III of Cyprus and Isabella of the House of Ibelin. [He is often but incorrectly called the Prince of Tyre.
2 CONC ]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1306, with the support of the barons, Amalric forced his brother Henry II to cede authority to him. He thereafter governed Cyprus as "rector, governor and administrator", effectively regent, until his assassination. Amalr
2 CONC ic was murdered by Simon of Montolif at Nicosia on 5 June 1310, and buried at Santa Sophia, at Nicosia.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1293 Amalric married Cilician Armenian Princess Isabella in Nicosia. Isabella was murdered in Armenia 9 April 1323. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh (d. betw 1318-1323, Cilicia), Lord of Crusoche, m. Eschiva of Ibelin (d. March 1324).
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry (murdered 9 April 1323)
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy (d. 17 April 1344), King of Armenia as Constantine II. He married Theodora Syrgiannaina. Their children were :
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle/Zampea of Poitiers-Lusignan
2 CONT John (murdered August 7, 1343, Cilicia)
2 CONT Bohémond (murdered April 17, 1344, Cilicia), Count of Corcyus, Lord of Korikos (1336), m. 1340 Euphemia of Neghir (1325-1381)
2 CONT Agnes (Mary) (d. 1309), m. c. 1305 Leo III of Armenia (1297-1307).
