0 @N6000000002187718653@ NOTE Antiochus Theos
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antiochus I Theos Dikaios Epiphanes Philorhomaios Philhellen (69–40 BC), was the most important king of the small Middle East kingdom of Commagene, which was situated in a region that is now in south-eastern Turkey and inhabited l
1 CONC argely by ethnic Kurds.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antiochus was the son of king Mithradates I Callinicus and the Seleucid princess Laodice, daughter of the Syrian King Antiochus VIII. This marriage had been arranged as part of a settlement by Mithradates' father Samos II to ensur
1 CONC e peace between the Kingdom of Commagene and the Seleucid Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antiochus tried to balance the interests of his kingdom with the reality of the world power of the Roman Empire and preserve as much as possible Commagene's independence. Despite his efforts, his reign ended with the Commagene sub
1 CONC mitting to Rome, becoming a client-state under EmperorAugustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anthiochus was a king who took his religion, a Hellenized form of Zoroastrianism, very seriously. He left behind a large number of Greek inscriptions, revealing many aspects of the royal cult he promoted. The kings of Commagene cl
1 CONC aimed descent from both the Persian and Selucid royal houses, therefore the gods they worshipped were a syncretism of Greek and Persian deities, some of them were personifications of the Sun, Moon and planets. He is said to nhave 
1 CONC practised astrology of a very esoteric kind, and layed the basis for a calendrical reform, by linking the Commagene year, which till then had been based on the movements of the Moon, to the Sothic (Star of Sirius) cycle used by th
1 CONC e Egyptians as the basis of their calendar. This would suggest that Antiochus was knowledgeable about, if not fully initiated into the Hermetic tradition.
1 CONT
1 CONT Today Antiochus is most famous for founding the impressive sanctuary of Nemrud Dagi, which is close to the city of Kahta on a road leading to Mount Nimrod. Its most important monument is his so-called Hierothesion, where his remai
1 CONC ns were buried. It was constructed not to be a simple tomb but as a place where he would gather his people for religious festivities connected with either his birthday or coronation. Forgotten for centuries, it was excavated in 18
1 CONC 83 by a German team of archeologists.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kahta, the gateway to the lost kingdom of Commagene, is situated north of the modern Turkish civil engineering project Ataturk Baraji (Dam), which distributes the waters of the Euphrates River. Unfortunately, the lake it formed dr
1 CONC owned Commagene's original capital of Samosata, built by Samos I in the early 3rd century B.C.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Litterature
1 CONT Adrian G. Gilbert, Magi, Bloomsbury Publishing Plc., 1996
0 @N6000000002187617024@ NOTE Adad-nirari II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Adad-nirari II is generally considered to be the first King of Assyria in the Neo-Assyrian period. He reigned from 912 to 891 BC. Because of the existence of full eponym lists from his reign down to the middle of the reign of Ashu
1 CONC rbanipal in the 7th century BC, year one of his reign in 911 BC is perhaps the first event in ancient Near Eastern history which can be dated to an exact year, although the Assyrian King List is generally considered to be quite ac
1 CONC curate for several centuries before Adad-nirari's reign, and scholars generally agree on a single set of dates back to Ashur-resh-ishi I in the late 12th century BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Adad-nirari succeeded his father, Ashur-dan II, and was succeeded by his son, Tukulti-Ninurta II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ashur-dan II King of Assyria
1 CONT 912–891 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Tukulti-Ninurta II 
0 @N6000000002187617043@ NOTE Ashur-dan II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashur-Dan II was King of Assyria. He succeeded his father, Tiglath-Pileser II, in 935 and reigned until his death in 912 BC, when he was succeeded by his son Adad-nirari II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Tiglath-Pileser II King of Assyria
1 CONT 934–912 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Adad-nirari II 
