0 @N6000000002187624780@ NOTE Iberia was a name given by the ancient Greeks and Romans to the ancient Georgian kingdom of Kartli (4th century BC-5th century AD) corresponding roughly to the eastern and southern parts of the present day Georgia.
1 CONT
1 CONT The term “Caucasian Iberia” (or the Eastern Iberia) is used to distinguish it from the Iberian Peninsula, where the present day nations of Spain and Portugal are located.
0 @N6000000002187624969@ NOTE Parnavaz II of Iberia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Parnavaz II (Bartom) (known as Pharnabazius to Romans) (d. 32 BC) was king of Caucasian Iberia (63-32 BC), the last in Iberian Arsacid line.
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded his father Artag who had surrendered to the Roman general Pompey in 65 BC. However, Roman power over Iberia proved to be impermanent. In 36 BC, legate Publius Canidius Crassus led his army against Iberians and brought
1 CONC  them to make an alliance against the Albanian king Zober (Zoberes). Canidius and Parnavaz marched to Albania and subdued its people.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 32 BC, he was dethroned and killed by the Parnavazian prince Mirian expelled from the country 60 years ago.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Cassius Dio. Roman History 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Artag King of Iberia
1 CONT 63 – 32 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Mirian II 
0 @N6000000002187624841@ NOTE Saurmag I of Iberia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Saurmag I (sometimes also spelled as Sauromaces, or Sayurmak) (??????? I) (d. ca 162 BC), from the House of Pharnavazians, was the 2nd king of Caucasian Iberia/Kartli in ca 237-162 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded on the death of his father, Pharnavaz I, ca 237 BC. There is some inconsistency in the dates of his reign, which seems implausibly long for this period.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Georgian annals, the nobles of the realm united to kill the king. Learning of the plot, Saurmag took refuge at his mother’s homeland, Dzurdzuk, in the Caucasus Mountains. Aided by the Dzurdzuk people, he crushed the r
1 CONC evolt and allowed part of these mountaineers to settle in the border regions of Iberia.
1 CONT
1 CONT He added two new goddesses, Ainina and Danina, to a Georgian pagan pantheon created by Pharnavaz I, and erected them idols on the road to the capital Mtskheta.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died in ca 162 BC, having had no son, and was succeeded by his son-in-law, Mirian I.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External link
1 CONT The Georgian Chronicle 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT c. Pharnavaz I King of Iberia
1 CONT c. 237 – c. 162 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Mirian I 
